            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Tamil for to plead, then the Tamil for to be fed up."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ஏங்கு",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ஏங்கு — ēṅku — \"to long for; to pine\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "வெளிநாட்டில் இருந்தபோது நான் அம்மாவின் சமையலுக்கு ஏங்கினேன். veḷināṭṭil iruntapōtu nāṉ ammāviṉ camaiyalukku ēṅkiṉēṉ. When I was abroad I longed for my mother’s cooking."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Past (I …): ஏங்கினேன், ēṅkiṉēṉ."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What one longs for takes -க்கு: அன்புக்கு ஏங்கு (ēṅku), to crave love."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ஏங்கு (ēṅku) is native Dravidian (DEDR 878); DEDR keeps a second ஏங்கு, \"to cry out\", as its own entry (879)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
